1
00:00:01,240 --> 00:00:02,860
Go outside tonight and look up.

2
00:00:03,020 --> 00:00:04,020
Between the stars,

3
00:00:04,040 --> 00:00:05,439
the sky is black.

4
00:00:06,289 --> 00:00:07,047
Obvious, right?

5
00:00:07,067 --> 00:00:08,020
The Sun has set,

6
00:00:08,040 --> 00:00:09,135
so it's dark.

7
00:00:09,575 --> 00:00:11,465
But for about four hundred years,

8
00:00:11,605 --> 00:00:14,777
that darkness was one of the great puzzles of astronomy.

9
00:00:15,657 --> 00:00:18,216
Because if the universe were endless, eternal,

10
00:00:18,236 --> 00:00:19,308
and full of stars,

11
00:00:19,328 --> 00:00:21,590
the night sky shouldn't be dark at all.

12
00:00:21,610 --> 00:00:22,532
It should blaze,

13
00:00:22,552 --> 00:00:23,624
in every direction,

14
00:00:23,644 --> 00:00:26,131
as bright as the surface of a star.

15
00:00:27,061 --> 00:00:29,365
The real answer turns out to be one of the

16
00:00:29,385 --> 00:00:32,401
best clues we have about where everything came from.

17
00:00:32,831 --> 00:00:35,013
So, why is the sky dark at night?

18
00:00:40,743 --> 00:00:41,603
Chapter one.

19
00:00:41,623 --> 00:00:43,097
A forest of stars.

20
00:00:43,797 --> 00:00:45,207
Here's the trick question:

21
00:00:45,387 --> 00:00:47,057
if the stars go on forever,

22
00:00:47,077 --> 00:00:48,818
where could you look and not see one?

23
00:00:49,248 --> 00:00:52,199
Imagine you're standing in the middle of a huge forest.

24
00:00:52,639 --> 00:00:53,932
Look in any direction,

25
00:00:53,952 --> 00:00:56,105
and your view ends on a tree trunk.

26
00:00:56,125 --> 00:00:57,368
Near trees look wide,

27
00:00:57,388 --> 00:00:58,580
far trees look thin.

28
00:00:58,600 --> 00:01:00,551
But if the forest is big enough,

29
00:01:00,571 --> 00:01:01,662
there are no gaps.

30
00:01:01,682 --> 00:01:02,662
Only wood.

31
00:01:03,422 --> 00:01:05,392
Now swap the trees for stars.

32
00:01:05,652 --> 00:01:07,212
If space went on forever,

33
00:01:07,252 --> 00:01:08,762
evenly filled with stars,

34
00:01:08,902 --> 00:01:10,343
every line of sight would end

35
00:01:10,363 --> 00:01:11,975
on the surface of a star.

36
00:01:12,425 --> 00:01:13,685
No black gaps.

37
00:01:13,865 --> 00:01:16,105
Just one continuous wall of starlight,

38
00:01:16,145 --> 00:01:17,125
day and night,

39
00:01:17,155 --> 00:01:19,067
as bright as the face of the Sun.

40
00:01:20,107 --> 00:01:23,057
Johannes Kepler worried about this in sixteen ten.

41
00:01:23,367 --> 00:01:26,099
Edmond Halley took it on in the seventeen twenties.

42
00:01:26,579 --> 00:01:28,139
But the puzzle is named after

43
00:01:28,159 --> 00:01:29,989
a German doctor and astronomer,

44
00:01:30,059 --> 00:01:33,399
Heinrich Olbers, who described it in eighteen twenty three.

45
00:01:33,769 --> 00:01:35,175
Olbers' paradox.

46
00:01:36,865 --> 00:01:37,775
Chapter two.

47
00:01:37,795 --> 00:01:39,145
Far away doesn't help.

48
00:01:39,835 --> 00:01:41,868
The obvious answer is that distant stars

49
00:01:41,888 --> 00:01:43,635
are just too faint to matter.

50
00:01:43,915 --> 00:01:46,018
Surprisingly, that doesn't work.

51
00:01:46,438 --> 00:01:49,258
Picture the stars arranged in shells around us,

52
00:01:49,328 --> 00:01:50,898
like the layers of an onion,

53
00:01:51,068 --> 00:01:53,004
every shell the same thickness.

54
00:01:53,444 --> 00:01:55,324
Take a shell twice as far away.

55
00:01:55,524 --> 00:01:57,985
Each star in it looks four times fainter.

56
00:01:58,415 --> 00:01:59,765
But that shell is bigger.

57
00:01:59,875 --> 00:02:02,091
It holds four times as many stars.

58
00:02:02,611 --> 00:02:03,911
Four times fainter,

59
00:02:03,931 --> 00:02:05,241
four times as many.

60
00:02:05,401 --> 00:02:06,981
They cancel out exactly.

61
00:02:07,191 --> 00:02:09,909
So every shell sends us the same amount of light.

62
00:02:10,759 --> 00:02:14,359
Add up shell after shell, forever,

63
00:02:14,379 --> 00:02:14,639
and the light just keeps piling up.

64
00:02:14,919 --> 00:02:17,179
Far away stars don't fade into nothing.

65
00:02:17,329 --> 00:02:18,660
They fill in the gaps.

66
00:02:19,100 --> 00:02:20,280
Do the sum properly,

67
00:02:20,300 --> 00:02:21,900
and the result is shocking.

68
00:02:22,230 --> 00:02:23,788
The whole sky should shine like

69
00:02:23,808 --> 00:02:25,170
the surface of the Sun,

70
00:02:25,190 --> 00:02:26,750
at thousands of degrees.

71
00:02:27,030 --> 00:02:27,960
Instead of a night,

72
00:02:28,010 --> 00:02:29,081
we'd get an oven.

73
00:02:30,861 --> 00:02:31,701
Chapter three.

74
00:02:31,721 --> 00:02:32,958
The wrong answers.

75
00:02:33,648 --> 00:02:35,878
So could something out there be blocking the light?

76
00:02:35,998 --> 00:02:37,378
It's a clever idea.

77
00:02:37,478 --> 00:02:38,722
It just doesn't work.

78
00:02:39,162 --> 00:02:41,719
Olbers himself thought that clouds of dust and

79
00:02:41,739 --> 00:02:44,343
gas might soak up the distant starlight.

80
00:02:44,783 --> 00:02:47,123
But anything that absorbs light warms up.

81
00:02:47,423 --> 00:02:48,623
Give it enough time,

82
00:02:48,713 --> 00:02:49,923
and the dust gets as hot

83
00:02:49,943 --> 00:02:51,463
as the starlight around it,

84
00:02:51,613 --> 00:02:53,782
and starts to glow just as brightly.

85
00:02:54,522 --> 00:02:56,993
The blocker becomes part of the wall of light.

86
00:02:57,823 --> 00:02:59,993
What about stars clumping into galaxies,

87
00:03:00,013 --> 00:03:01,703
with empty space in between?

88
00:03:01,943 --> 00:03:04,113
That only rearranges the forest.

89
00:03:04,313 --> 00:03:05,633
If it goes on forever,

90
00:03:05,653 --> 00:03:07,131
the gaps still close.

91
00:03:07,571 --> 00:03:10,469
Something more basic had to be wrong with the picture.

92
00:03:12,239 --> 00:03:13,149
Chapter four.

93
00:03:13,169 --> 00:03:14,475
A poet's answer.

94
00:03:15,165 --> 00:03:16,726
The first person to guess the

95
00:03:16,746 --> 00:03:18,665
right answer wasn't an astronomer.

96
00:03:18,905 --> 00:03:19,983
He was a poet.

97
00:03:20,423 --> 00:03:21,868
In eighteen forty eight,

98
00:03:21,888 --> 00:03:22,862
Edgar Allan Poe,

99
00:03:22,882 --> 00:03:24,380
the writer of The Raven,

100
00:03:24,400 --> 00:03:25,427
published a long,

101
00:03:25,447 --> 00:03:28,633
strange essay about the universe, called Eureka.

102
00:03:29,073 --> 00:03:32,263
In it,

103
00:03:32,363 --> 00:03:33,529
he suggested the background could be so far away

104
00:03:33,549 --> 00:03:34,936
that its light simply hasn't reached us yet.

105
00:03:35,386 --> 00:03:36,466
Light is fast,

106
00:03:36,486 --> 00:03:38,156
but not infinitely fast.

107
00:03:38,346 --> 00:03:40,578
And the universe hasn't been around forever.

108
00:03:41,038 --> 00:03:42,118
When you look at the Moon,

109
00:03:42,138 --> 00:03:44,228
you see it as it was about a second ago.

110
00:03:44,588 --> 00:03:46,768
The Sun, as it was eight minutes ago.

111
00:03:47,008 --> 00:03:48,518
The Andromeda galaxy,

112
00:03:48,538 --> 00:03:50,380
two and a half million years ago.

113
00:03:51,210 --> 00:03:53,980
It's about thirteen point eight billion years old.

114
00:03:54,190 --> 00:03:55,628
So we can only see light that

115
00:03:55,648 --> 00:03:57,120
has had time to get here.

116
00:03:57,390 --> 00:03:59,878
Everything farther out is still on its way.

117
00:04:00,318 --> 00:04:03,538
Inside that bubble there are an enormous number of stars.

118
00:04:03,708 --> 00:04:06,158
But not nearly enough to cover the whole sky.

119
00:04:06,408 --> 00:04:08,336
Between them, there are gaps.

120
00:04:08,786 --> 00:04:11,256
Our telescopes can see deep into that bubble.

121
00:04:11,656 --> 00:04:14,605
Webb has spotted galaxies whose light left them less

122
00:04:14,625 --> 00:04:17,903
than three hundred million years after the Big Bang.

123
00:04:18,633 --> 00:04:19,713
In nineteen oh one,

124
00:04:19,733 --> 00:04:22,883
Lord Kelvin did the math and showed this really works.

125
00:04:23,173 --> 00:04:24,383
To fill every gap,

126
00:04:24,433 --> 00:04:26,007
light would need around ten to

127
00:04:26,027 --> 00:04:27,913
the power of twenty three years.

128
00:04:28,173 --> 00:04:30,773
The universe is trillions of times too young.

129
00:04:30,973 --> 00:04:33,637
And stars burn out long before that anyway.

130
00:04:35,407 --> 00:04:36,307
Chapter five.

131
00:04:36,327 --> 00:04:37,515
The glowing wall.

132
00:04:38,215 --> 00:04:39,355
Here's the twist:

133
00:04:39,415 --> 00:04:39,955
in a way,

134
00:04:39,975 --> 00:04:41,165
Olbers was right.

135
00:04:41,365 --> 00:04:44,089
There really is a glowing wall behind the stars.

136
00:04:44,529 --> 00:04:46,009
Space is expanding.

137
00:04:46,279 --> 00:04:47,509
As light crosses it,

138
00:04:47,529 --> 00:04:48,969
its waves get stretched,

139
00:04:48,989 --> 00:04:50,989
so the light gets redder and weaker.

140
00:04:51,249 --> 00:04:53,849
That makes distant galaxies even dimmer.

141
00:04:54,279 --> 00:04:56,231
It's one reason the James Webb

142
00:04:56,251 --> 00:04:58,639
Space Telescope looks in infrared.

143
00:04:59,059 --> 00:05:01,471
The light of the first galaxies has been stretched

144
00:05:01,491 --> 00:05:03,907
right out of the colours our eyes can see.

145
00:05:04,357 --> 00:05:06,107
Now run the clock backwards.

146
00:05:06,357 --> 00:05:10,217
Early on, the whole universe was filled with hot,

147
00:05:10,237 --> 00:05:12,117
glowing gas, at about three thousand degrees.

148
00:05:12,347 --> 00:05:14,221
Like the surface of a cool star.

149
00:05:14,651 --> 00:05:17,611
Back then, the sky really was bright in every direction.

150
00:05:17,831 --> 00:05:19,799
Olbers' wall of light was real.

151
00:05:20,769 --> 00:05:22,686
About three hundred and eighty thousand

152
00:05:22,706 --> 00:05:24,219
years after the Big Bang,

153
00:05:24,309 --> 00:05:26,799
the gas cooled enough for light to fly free.

154
00:05:27,169 --> 00:05:28,839
That light is still arriving,

155
00:05:28,859 --> 00:05:30,229
from every direction.

156
00:05:30,679 --> 00:05:31,914
But by now it has been

157
00:05:31,934 --> 00:05:33,829
stretched about a thousand times.

158
00:05:34,079 --> 00:05:37,319
It has cooled to under three degrees above absolute zero.

159
00:05:37,579 --> 00:05:39,269
It isn't visible light anymore.

160
00:05:39,399 --> 00:05:40,735
It's microwaves.

161
00:05:41,465 --> 00:05:42,905
In nineteen sixty four,

162
00:05:42,925 --> 00:05:44,835
two scientists at Bell Labs,

163
00:05:44,895 --> 00:05:47,075
Arno Penzias and Robert Wilson,

164
00:05:47,155 --> 00:05:48,560
heard a strange hiss in their

165
00:05:48,580 --> 00:05:50,375
antenna that came from everywhere.

166
00:05:50,635 --> 00:05:52,267
They even cleaned out pigeon droppings

167
00:05:52,287 --> 00:05:53,345
to get rid of it.

168
00:05:53,675 --> 00:05:54,595
The hiss stayed.

169
00:05:54,785 --> 00:05:55,999
It was this glow.

170
00:05:57,069 --> 00:05:59,029
If your eyes could see microwaves,

171
00:05:59,059 --> 00:06:00,989
the night sky wouldn't be dark at all.

172
00:06:01,369 --> 00:06:02,329
It would glow,

173
00:06:02,349 --> 00:06:03,899
almost perfectly evenly,

174
00:06:03,919 --> 00:06:05,036
everywhere you look.

175
00:06:06,816 --> 00:06:07,786
Chapter six.

176
00:06:07,806 --> 00:06:09,319
The darkness we're losing.

177
00:06:10,009 --> 00:06:11,208
So the dark sky is a

178
00:06:11,228 --> 00:06:13,149
clue to how the universe began.

179
00:06:13,409 --> 00:06:15,936
And most of us can't really see it anymore.

180
00:06:16,376 --> 00:06:17,536
In twenty sixteen,

181
00:06:17,696 --> 00:06:19,842
a world atlas of light pollution found

182
00:06:19,862 --> 00:06:21,820
that more than a third of humanity

183
00:06:21,840 --> 00:06:23,926
can no longer see the Milky Way.

184
00:06:24,416 --> 00:06:27,573
That includes nearly eighty percent of North Americans.

185
00:06:28,033 --> 00:06:29,573
From a truly dark place,

186
00:06:29,603 --> 00:06:30,955
you can see around two and

187
00:06:30,975 --> 00:06:32,723
a half thousand stars at once.

188
00:06:33,043 --> 00:06:34,433
From the middle of a big city,

189
00:06:34,483 --> 00:06:36,180
often only a few dozen.

190
00:06:36,610 --> 00:06:37,920
And it's getting worse.

191
00:06:38,250 --> 00:06:40,802
More than fifty thousand people counting stars

192
00:06:40,822 --> 00:06:42,707
by eye showed that the night sky

193
00:06:42,727 --> 00:06:45,310
got about ten percent brighter every year,

194
00:06:45,450 --> 00:06:47,689
from twenty eleven to twenty twenty two.

195
00:06:48,109 --> 00:06:52,349
At that rate,

196
00:06:52,489 --> 00:06:53,372
a child born where two hundred and

197
00:06:53,392 --> 00:06:54,361
fifty stars are visible would see only

198
00:06:54,381 --> 00:06:55,869
about a hundred by their eighteenth birthday.

199
00:06:56,749 --> 00:06:58,289
And it's not only about the view.

200
00:06:58,619 --> 00:07:01,539
Bright lights at night confuse migrating birds,

201
00:07:01,589 --> 00:07:04,535
and lead newly hatched sea turtles away from the sea.

202
00:07:05,275 --> 00:07:06,541
The fixes are simple.

203
00:07:06,561 --> 00:07:09,215
Shielded lights that point down, warmer bulbs,

204
00:07:09,235 --> 00:07:11,736
and switching off what nobody needs.

205
00:07:12,666 --> 00:07:14,506
So, why is the sky dark at night?

206
00:07:14,526 --> 00:07:16,726
Not just because the Sun has set.

207
00:07:17,556 --> 00:07:20,046
It's dark because the universe had a beginning.

208
00:07:20,276 --> 00:07:22,366
Because light needs time to travel.

209
00:07:22,606 --> 00:07:24,395
And because space keeps stretching that

210
00:07:24,415 --> 00:07:25,883
light on its way to us.

211
00:07:26,613 --> 00:07:29,353
Every patch of black between the stars is a clue.

212
00:07:29,663 --> 00:07:30,713
When you look into it,

213
00:07:30,813 --> 00:07:33,062
you're looking back toward the beginning of time.

214
00:07:33,992 --> 00:07:36,552
Olbers asked a question a child could ask.

215
00:07:36,862 --> 00:07:39,362
Answering it took a poet, a physicist,

216
00:07:39,382 --> 00:07:40,902
two radio scientists,

217
00:07:40,922 --> 00:07:42,767
and a pile of pigeon droppings.

218
00:07:43,607 --> 00:07:46,897
The Leonid meteor shower peaks around November seventeenth.

219
00:07:47,357 --> 00:07:49,247
Find a dark spot, look up,

220
00:07:49,267 --> 00:07:50,814
and enjoy the darkness.

221
00:07:51,744 --> 00:07:53,494
If you enjoyed this,

222
00:07:53,514 --> 00:07:55,084
give it a like,

223
00:07:55,364 --> 00:07:56,744
and subscribe to Whyverse.

224
00:07:56,824 --> 00:07:58,514
And for shorter stories,

225
00:07:58,534 --> 00:07:58,989
find us on Instagram and TikTok.

